  is the username; the field exists so the later server value has somewhere to land without changing the shape.

  Error handling is where most of the real design is, and it rests on one invariant:

  [2m▎[0m [1;3m[38;5;153mgetCurrentUser()[39m never throws. [38;5;153mnull[39m means "no usable identity."[0m

  That collapses three different failures into the one case consumers already have to handle — not logged in. A future
  form does [38;5;153mconst u = getCurrentUser(); if (!u) ...[39m and is correct in all of them. The three:

  - [1mStorage unavailable or throwing.[0m Safari private mode has historically thrown on [38;5;153msetItem[39m; storage can be
    policy-disabled; writes can hit quota. Every storage call in [38;5;153msession.mjs[39m is wrapped. A failed [3mwrite[0m is warned and
    swallowed — the login genuinely succeeded, and failing it because storage is full would be the worse outcome. A
    failed [3mread[0m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m.
  - [1mCorrupt JSON.[0m [38;5;153mJSON.parse[39m throws → catch, [38;5;153mclearCurrentUser()[39m, return [38;5;153mnull[39m. Self-healing rather than permanently
    broken.
  - [1mWrong shape.[0m Parsed but missing a non-empty string [38;5;153muserId[39m → same treatment. This one isn't paranoia: [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m
    is shared by everything on the origin, so a key collision is a real way to get valid JSON that isn't ours.

  Two more, outside [38;5;153msession.mjs[39m:

  - [1m[38;5;153mlogin[39m rejects.[0m The stub can't today — that's the whole point of making the interface async now. [38;5;153mapp.mjs[39m wraps the
    call in try/catch and logs. Without it, an async submit handler turns a failure into an unhandled rejection and the
    user sees nothing at all.
  - [1mFailed login must not leave a stale identity.[0m If someone is logged in as A and a later attempt fails, the session
    still says A. So [38;5;153mlogin[39m calls [38;5;153mclearCurrentUser()[39m on any failure or rejection, giving: [3mthe session never holds an [0m
    [3midentity that the most recent authentication attempt didn't establish.[0m

  One thing going into the spec as an explicit non-goal: this identity is [1mself-asserted convenience data, not [0m
  [1mauthorization[0m. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m verifies nothing, and [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m is writable by any script on the origin. Stamping a form
  with it is fine; gating anything on it is not, and the server must re-verify independently.

  Does the error handling look right — particularly clearing on failed login, and swallowing write failures rather than
  surfacing them?

